From: gaokun-android contributors <nekovahiru@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/crtc: drop the racy BUG_ON in fence_to_crtc()

fence_to_crtc() re-checks fence->ops after the caller has already
dereferenced it:

	static struct drm_crtc *fence_to_crtc(struct dma_fence *fence)
	{
		BUG_ON(rcu_access_pointer(fence->ops) != &drm_crtc_fence_ops);
		return container_of(fence->extern_lock, struct drm_crtc, fence_lock);
	}

Since commit 541c8f2468b9 ("dma-buf: detach fence ops on signal v3") that
re-check is racy, and losing the race kills the machine.

dma_fence_signal_timestamp_locked() detaches the ops of any fence whose ops
provide neither ->release nor ->wait:

	ops = rcu_dereference_protected(fence->ops, true);
	if (!ops->release && !ops->wait)
		RCU_INIT_POINTER(fence->ops, NULL);

drm_crtc_fence_ops provides exactly ->get_driver_name and ->get_timeline_name
and nothing else, so every CRTC fence is detached the moment it signals.

Meanwhile dma_fence_driver_name() and dma_fence_timeline_name() load the ops
once and then call through them:

	ops = rcu_dereference(fence->ops);
	if (ops)
		return (const char __rcu *)ops->get_driver_name(fence);

so there is a window between that load and the BUG_ON re-read. If the fence
signals inside it -- and a CRTC out-fence signals on every vblank -- the
BUG_ON observes NULL and takes the whole kernel down:

  kernel BUG at drivers/gpu/drm/drm_crtc.c:161!
  Internal error: Oops - BUG: 00000000f2000800 [#1] SMP
  CPU: 1 UID: 1000 PID: 1658 Comm: RenderThread 7.2.0-rc2 PREEMPT
  pc : drm_crtc_fence_get_driver_name+0x2c/0x30
  lr : dma_fence_driver_name+0x1c/0x34
  Call trace:
   drm_crtc_fence_get_driver_name+0x2c/0x30 (P)
   sync_file_ioctl+0x260/0x610
   __arm64_sys_ioctl+0xac/0x104
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops - BUG: Fatal exception

Observed twice within five minutes on a Huawei MateBook E Go (SC8280XP, MSM
DPU, 120 Hz panel) running Android 16, once through dma_fence_driver_name()
as above and once through dma_fence_timeline_name() ->
drm_crtc_fence_get_timeline_name() -> sync_file_get_name(). Both times the
caller was an ordinary application's RenderThread doing SYNC_IOC_FILE_INFO on
a present fence, so this is a kernel panic reachable by unprivileged
userspace on any driver that hands CRTC out-fences to applications.

Deleting the check loses nothing. fence_to_crtc() has exactly two callers,
both of them members of drm_crtc_fence_ops, so they are only reachable when
the ops did match at dereference time -- which is the very property the
BUG_ON tries to assert, and the only moment at which it can be asserted
without a race.

What the callbacks go on to read stays valid: crtc->timeline_name is an array
embedded in the CRTC and crtc->dev->driver->name is static storage. Detaching
the ops only announces that driver data may be freed after an RCU grace
period, and both accessors are documented as callable only inside
rcu_read_lock(), which is what keeps the CRTC itself alive.

Fixes: 541c8f2468b9 ("dma-buf: detach fence ops on signal v3")

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_crtc.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_crtc.c
@@ -158,7 +158,6 @@ static const struct dma_fence_ops drm_crtc_fence_ops;
 
 static struct drm_crtc *fence_to_crtc(struct dma_fence *fence)
 {
-	BUG_ON(rcu_access_pointer(fence->ops) != &drm_crtc_fence_ops);
 	return container_of(fence->extern_lock, struct drm_crtc, fence_lock);
 }
 
